<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustavo Álvarez Martínez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustavo Álvarez Martínez (1937–1989) was a Honduran general who commanded the armed forces from 1982 to 1984 and was the most powerful figure in the country during the early years of US-backed operations against Sandinista <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, he oversaw a campaign of forced disappearances of leftists carried out partly by the army's Battalion 3-16. Fellow officers removed him in 1984, and he was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a> in 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustavo-alvarez-martinez.jpg" alt="Gustavo Álvarez Martínez" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JVC3ETA · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustavo_Adolfo_Alvarez_Martinez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gustavo Adolfo Álvarez Martínez was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tela">Tela</a> on 12 December 1937, the brother of Armando Álvarez Martínez, later Minister of Culture under President Policarpo Paz García. He trained at the Colegio Militar de la Nación in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and graduated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/school-of-the-americas">School of the Americas</a> in 1976. He later commanded the Fuerza de Seguridad Publica (FUSEP), the national police and a branch of the military, under which Battalion 3-16 was first formed with trainers from Argentina whom he invited.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In February 1981, already chosen to head the armed forces, he told the US ambassador Jack R. Binns that extralegal methods might be needed against subversives and praised the Argentine method, which Binns took to mean the kidnapping and disappearance of government opponents. His chief of staff, General José Bueso Rosa, said the idea of an intelligence unit reporting to the head of the armed forces had come from the US side, and CIA training of the battalion was later confirmed in testimony to the Senate. In 1983 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> awarded him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Merit.</p><p>A 1994 report by Oscar Valladares, appointed by the Honduran parliament to investigate abuses, blamed the army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contras">Contras</a> for 174 disappearances and kidnappings in the 1980s, most of them before March 1984. A 1996 CIA report found that the Honduran military had committed most of the hundreds of abuses reported between 1980 and 1984. Florencio Caballero, a former member of Battalion 3-16, testified that Álvarez had ordered the death of the American missionary Father James Carney.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fellow generals accusing him of abuses removed him on 31 March 1984, when he sought to extend his control over the armed forces, and sent him into exile. He worked as a consultant to the Pentagon and lived in Miami until 1988, when he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. He had also been president of the Asociación para el Progreso de Honduras (APROH), which linked business leaders and the military. He was assassinated by leftwing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> on 25 January 1989.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gustavo Álvarez Martínez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.11,-87.21&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Tegucigalpa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2163 1638 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gustavo Álvarez Martínez"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2613 1908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2613 1908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.11,-87.21&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
